<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">orientable Poincaré duality group</span> <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is a group of type <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FP</annotation></semantics></math> whose homology and cohomology are associated via the strong property that</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{H}^{i}(\Gamma;A)\cong\mathrm{H}_{d-i}(\Gamma;A)\ \ \ \mbox{for any $\Gamma$-module $A$ and $i\in\mathbf{Z}$.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.5em;" width="1.5em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>for any </mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mtext>-module </mtext><mi>A</mi><mtext> and </mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{H}^{i}(\Gamma;A)\cong\mathrm{H}_{d-i}(\Gamma;A)\ \ \ \mbox{for any $\Gamma$-module $A$ and $i\in\mathbf{Z}$.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Unfortunately, many important groups do not even have finite index subgroups which are orientable Poincaré duality groups. For example, free groups on two or more generators, fundamental groups of nontrivial knot complements, braid groups on three or more strands, <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and mapping class groups of surfaces of genus <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> do not have finite index subgroups which are orientable Poincaré duality groups. However, each of these groups does have a finite index subgroup which is a <span id="S1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Bieri-Eckmann duality group</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">BE73</a>]</cite> (see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Iv02</a>, §6.1]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Br82</a>, §VIII.10]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bi76</a>, §9]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A group <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> of type <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FP</annotation></semantics></math> is a Bieri-Eckmann duality group, or simply a <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">duality group</span>, of dimension <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> if there is a <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> we have</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{H}^{i}(\Gamma;A)\cong\mathrm{H}_{d-i}(\Gamma;D\otimes_{\mathbf{Z}}A)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></msub><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{H}^{i}(\Gamma;A)\cong\mathrm{H}_{d-i}(\Gamma;D\otimes_{\mathbf{Z}}A).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\otimes_{\mathbf{Z}}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></msub><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\otimes_{\mathbf{Z}}A</annotation></semantics></math> with the diagonal action: <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(x\otimes a)=(\gamma x)\otimes(\gamma a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(x\otimes a)=(\gamma x)\otimes(\gamma a)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in D</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in A</annotation></semantics></math>.
This <math id="S1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by the duality group <math id="S1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and is called the <span id="S1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dualizing module</span> of <math id="S1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the dualizing module is a fundamental cohomological object associated to any duality group. It is, in fact, expressible as the top nontrival cohomology group of <math id="S1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> with coefficients in the group ring <math id="S1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence the dualizing module of the duality group <math id="S1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> with cohomological dimension <math id="S1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-module</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\cong\mathrm{H}^{d}(\Gamma;\mathbf{Z}\Gamma)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\cong\mathrm{H}^{d}(\Gamma;\mathbf{Z}\Gamma).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Orientable Poincaré duality groups may be characterized as duality groups for which the dualizing module is the trivial module <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case Equation (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) simplifies to Equation (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Therefore, one may view the dualizing module of <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> as a sort of “error term” measuring the extent to which a duality group fails to be a Poincaré duality group.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> be a group with finite index subgroups <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of finite cohomological dimensions <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. Then <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}=d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}=d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is said to have <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">virtual cohomological dimension</span> <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{vcd}(\Gamma)=d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{vcd}(\Gamma)=d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">There is a similar invariance of dualizing modules for finite index subgroups. Let <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> be a group with finite index subgroups <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> which are duality groups with dualizing modules <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. Then <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1}\cong D_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1}\cong D_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Gamma_{1}\cap\Gamma_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Gamma_{1}\cap\Gamma_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. That is,</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Res}^{\Gamma_{1}}_{\Gamma_{1}\cap\Gamma_{2}}(D_{1})\cong\mathrm{Res}^{\Gamma_{2}}_{\Gamma_{1}\cap\Gamma_{2}}(D_{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Res</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Res</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Res}^{\Gamma_{1}}_{\Gamma_{1}\cap\Gamma_{2}}(D_{1})\cong\mathrm{Res}^{\Gamma_{2}}_{\Gamma_{1}\cap\Gamma_{2}}(D_{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> denote either the closed genus <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> surface <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> or that surface <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with a marked point. Let <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> be the mapping class group of <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. Harvey’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hv81</a>, §2]</cite> complex of curves <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> (see Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 2.1 (Harvey []). ‣ 2 The Steinberg module, the curve complex and the arc complex ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> below) is a simplicial complex with a natural simplicial action of <math id="S1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>. Harer has shown that the curve complex has the homotopy type of a wedge sum of spheres of dimension <math id="S1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2</annotation></semantics></math>. The <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Steinberg module</span> is the <math id="S1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>-module</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma)=\mathrm{H}_{2g-2}(\mathcal{C}(\Sigma);\mathbf{Z})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma)=\mathrm{H}_{2g-2}(\mathcal{C}(\Sigma);\mathbf{Z}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Harer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha86</a>, Theorem 4.1]</cite> and Ivanov <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Iv87</a>, Theorem 6.6]</cite> in the case of the closed surface have shown that the mapping class group <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> is a virtual duality group, and that the dualizing module for any torsion-free, finite index subgroup of <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Steinberg module <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus this module is a fundamental cohomological object associated to the commensurability class of <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Summary of results</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Again, let <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\in\{\Sigma_{g},\Sigma_{g}^{1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\in\{\Sigma_{g},\Sigma_{g}^{1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The purpose of this paper is to initiate an investigation of the (left) <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>-module structure of the Steinberg module.</p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma)=\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{2g-2}(\mathcal{C}(\Sigma);\mathbf{Z})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma)=\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{2g-2}(\mathcal{C}(\Sigma);\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Although Harer has proven that the curve complex <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> has the homotopy type of a wedge sum of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2</annotation></semantics></math> spheres, no homologically nontrivial sphere was previously known.
We show that <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> has a large finite generating set with a generator for each topologically distinct way of gluing the sides of a <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4g</annotation></semantics></math>-gon to get a surface of genus <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. We show that the standard identification of sides of the <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4g</annotation></semantics></math>-gon results is a trivial class in <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> (see Remark <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Remark 4.4. ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> below), but Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Corollary 4.3. ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> gives another identification of sides <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> which gives a nontrivial class in <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>. This is our first main result</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_mainthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Main Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The homology class <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\phi_{0}]\in\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\phi_{0}]\in\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\phi_{0}]\in\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\phi_{0}]\in\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>) is nontrivial for <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The number of topologically distinct ways of gluing the sides of a <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4g</annotation></semantics></math>-gon to get a surface of genus <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> grows quickly in <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the first finite <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>-module generating set which we give in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 3.6. ‣ 3 A resolution of the Steinberg module ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a> below is quite large. It is therefore somewhat surprising that in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2 (The Steinberg module is cyclic). ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> below, we show that this large finite generating set may be reduced to a singleton. This is our second main result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_mainthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Main Theorem 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> the Steinberg module <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> is a cyclic <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>-module generated by the single element described in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Main Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Main Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1.1 Summary of results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> may be compared to a result of Ash-Rudolph <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">AR79</a>, Theorem 4.1]</cite> which implies that the reduced homology of the Tits building for <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is a cyclic <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>-module.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>The curve complex and duality for mapping class groups</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We briefly overview the reason that the reduced homology of the curve complex is the dualizing module for any torsion-free, finite index subgroup of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>. The mapping class group acts properly discontinuously on Teichmüller space <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is diffeomorphic to <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}^{6g-6+2m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}^{6g-6+2m}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of marked points on <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. For any torsion-free, finite index subgroup <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma&lt;\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma&lt;\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> the quotient of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> by the properly discontinuous, fixed-point-free action of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is a manifold. This manifold is not compact but can be compactified either by adding certain “points at infinity” following Ivanov <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Iv89</a>]</cite> or by removing a certain open neighborhood of infinity following Harer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha86</a>]</cite>. In either case, the universal cover <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> of this compactified manifold is called a <em id="S1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bordification</em> of Teichmüller space. One must then show that <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> is contractible and that <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\overline{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\overline{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> has the homotopy type of a wedge of spheres of dimension <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2</annotation></semantics></math>. It then follows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">BE73</a>, §6.4]</cite> that the cohomological dimension of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6g-6+2m-(2g-2)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6g-6+2m-(2g-2)-1</annotation></semantics></math> and the dualizing module of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{2g-2}(\partial\overline{\mathcal{T}};\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{2g-2}(\partial\overline{\mathcal{T}};\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>. Harer establishes these results for surfaces with boundary or punctures in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha86</a>]</cite>. In addition, he shows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha86</a>, Lemma 3.2]</cite> that <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\overline{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\overline{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariantly homotopy equivalent to the curve complex. Ivanov <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Iv87</a>]</cite> establishes the same for closed surfaces.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3 </span>The Tits building for <math id="S1.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout this paper we will maintain the viewpoint that the homology of the curve complex should be viewed as an analog for the mapping class group of the homology of the rational Tits building for <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>. Briefly, the rational Tits building <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is the simplicial complex of flags of nontrivial proper subspaces of <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Br89</a>, §V.1 Example 1B]</cite>). The action of <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a simplicial action of <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>. For any basis <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> the union of all simplices of <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> whose vertices are subspaces spanned by nonempty proper subsets of <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}</annotation></semantics></math> gives an <span id="S1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">apartment</span> of <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>. Apartments have the homeomorphism type of an <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-sphere.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Steinberg module for <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">BS73</a>, pg. 437]</cite> to be the infinitely generated free abelian group</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(n)=\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{n-2}(\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q});\mathbf{Z})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(n)=\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{n-2}(\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q});\mathbf{Z}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Borel and Serre <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">BS73</a>, Theorem 11.4.2]</cite> show that the dualizing module of any torsion-free finite index subgroup of <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> providing inspiration for Harer’s later work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha86</a>]</cite> on the mapping class group.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The Solomon-Tits Theorem (<span id="S1.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cf.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">So69</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Br89</a>, §IV.5 Theorem 2]</cite>) states two things. Firstly, it says that the Tits building <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> has the homotopy type of a wedge of infinitely many <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-spheres. Secondly, it says that <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(n)=\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{n-2}(\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q});\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(n)=\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{n-2}(\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q});\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is spanned by the homology classes of all apartments. The action of <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is transitive on the set of apartments of <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> so one sees immediately that <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{n-2}(\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q});\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{n-2}(\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q});\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is a cyclic <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>-module.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In analogy with the first part of the Solomon-Tits Theorem, Harer has shown that the curve complex has the homotopy type of a wedge of <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2g-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2g-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-spheres.
We prove the analog of the second part in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2 (The Steinberg module is cyclic). ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> below which states that as a <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>-module the Steinberg module <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by a single element.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Actually, Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2 (The Steinberg module is cyclic). ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> more closely resembles a result of Ash-Rudolph <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">AR79</a>, Theorem 4.1]</cite> which implies that the reduced homology of the Tits building <math id="S1.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is a cyclic <math id="S1.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>-module. The action of <math id="S1.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is no longer transitive on the set of apartments of <math id="S1.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>, so Ash and Rudolph give a reduction process to rewrite the homology class of the sphere for an arbitrary apartment as a sum of homology classes of spheres of “integral unimodular” apartments. Our analogous reduction process is given in Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 4.6. ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a> below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S2" title="2 The Steinberg module, the curve complex and the arc complex ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> below we summarize the results of Harer and others on which or current discussion relys. In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 A resolution of the Steinberg module ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> we derive a resolution of the Steinberg module which yields a large finite generating set. Finally, in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> we show that the Steinberg module is generated by a single element.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acknowledgements.</span>  I owe a huge debt to Juan Souto who pointed out that the best way to find the spheres in the curve complex is to look for balls they bound in Teichmüller space. I am also indebted to Karen Vogtmann for elucidating the work of Harer. Benson Farb provided immeasurable support and feedback for this project. I would also like to thank Mladen Bestvina, Matthew Day, Justin Malestein, and Robert Penner for some very helpful discussions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>The Steinberg module, the curve complex and the arc complex</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the surface of genus <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the surface of genus <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> marked point <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ast" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ast</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mapping class group</span> <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>) is the group of orientation-preserving self diffeomorphisms of <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. orientation-preserving self diffeomorphisms of <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> fixing the marked point) modulo diffeomorphisms isotopic to the identity (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Iv02</a>]</cite> for a survey). An <em id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(essential) curve</em> in <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isotopy class of the image of an embedding of the circle <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> not bounding a disk in <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. An <em id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(essential) curve</em> in <math id="S2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isotopy class of the image of an embedding of <math id="S2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}\smallsetminus\{\ast\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}\smallsetminus\{\ast\}</annotation></semantics></math> not bounding a disk or a once-punctured disk in <math id="S2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}\smallsetminus\{\ast\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}\smallsetminus\{\ast\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that for the surface with a marked point, curves may not be isotoped past the marked point. In either surface, a <span id="S2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">curve system</span> is a set of curves (with isotopy class representatives) which can be made to be disjoint. Since curves are isotopy classes, a curve system cannot have parallel curves. We can partially order curve systems by inclusion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Harvey <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hv81</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> the <span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">curve complex</span> <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>) for the surface <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>) is the simplicial complex with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-simplices corresponding to curve systems with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> curves and face relation given by inclusion.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Both <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> have the homotopy type of an infinite wedge of spheres of dimension <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha86</a>, Theorem 3.5]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">IJ08</a>, Theorem 1.4]</cite>). As stated above, the reduced homology of the curve complex (which is concentrated in dimension <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2</annotation></semantics></math>) is fundamental to the structure of the mapping class group as a virtual duality group with virtual cohomological dimension <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4g-5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4g-5</annotation></semantics></math> for the closed surface and dimension <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4g-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4g-3</annotation></semantics></math> for the surface with one marked point <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha86</a>, Theorem 4.1]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> the <span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Steinberg module</span> for the mapping class group <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-module</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1}):=\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{2g-2}(\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1});\mathbf{Z})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1}):=\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{2g-2}(\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1});\mathbf{Z}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and the <span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Steinberg module</span> for the mapping class group <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>-module</p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}):=\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{2g-2}(\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g});\mathbf{Z})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}):=\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{2g-2}(\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g});\mathbf{Z}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our aim is to investigate the module structure of the Steinberg modules <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. A result of Harer cuts our work in half. The mapping class group <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> acts on the curve complex <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> by forgetting the marked point. Hence <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is naturally a <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-module. Harer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha86</a>, Lemma 3.6]</cite> has shown that forgetting the marked point gives a <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant homotopy equivalence <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\simeq\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\simeq\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. See the work of Kent, Leininger, and Schleimer in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">KLS09</a>]</cite> for more on this projection of curve complexes. We therefore have the following lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Harer)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">As <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-modules <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\cong\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\cong\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This in turn allows an immediate conclusion about the <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-module structure of <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> which is not at all apparent from direct observation of the action of <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The action of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> factors through its quotient <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In light of Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 2.3 (Harer). ‣ 2 The Steinberg module, the curve complex and the arc complex ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> we will focus exclusively on the <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-module structure of <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Instead of calculating the homology of the curve complex <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> directly, we will work in the <span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">arc complex</span> for <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 2.5 (Harer). ‣ 2 The Steinberg module, the curve complex and the arc complex ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> below). Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 2.3 (Harer). ‣ 2 The Steinberg module, the curve complex and the arc complex ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> is especially fortunate since no structure analogous to the arc complex is readily available for the surface <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">An <span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(essential) arc</span> in <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isotopy class of the image of an embedded loop based at the marked point <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ast" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ast</annotation></semantics></math> which does not bound a disk in <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
An <span id="S2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">arc system</span> is a set of arcs (with isotopy class representatives) which intersect only at the marked point. As with curve systems, since arc arc systems are sets of isotopy classes, arc systems may not have parallel arcs. Henceforth we will make no distinction between an arc system and a set of representatives of each arc intersecting only at the marked point.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Harer)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">arc complex</span> <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=\mathcal{A}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=\mathcal{A}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is the simplicial complex with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-simplices corresponding to arc systems with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> arcs and face relation given by inclusion.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">An arc system <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\{\alpha_{0},\cdots,\alpha_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\{\alpha_{0},\cdots,\alpha_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fills</span> <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> if the connected components of <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}\smallsetminus\bigcup\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em">∖</mo><mrow><mo>⋃</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}\smallsetminus\bigcup\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> are all disks. The minimum number of arcs needed to fill <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g</annotation></semantics></math>. In what follows we will want to keep careful track of the number of arcs in a filling system, so we will say that a filling arc system <em id="S2.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-fills</em> <math id="S2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> if the arc system has <math id="S2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+k</annotation></semantics></math> arcs. Notice that a <math id="S2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system cuts the surface into <math id="S2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> disks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Harer)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">arc complex at infinity</span> <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{\infty}=\mathcal{A}_{\infty}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{\infty}=\mathcal{A}_{\infty}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is the simplicial subcomplex of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> which is the union of the simplices of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> whose vertex sets do not fill <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Any arc system with fewer than <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g</annotation></semantics></math> arcs cannot fill <math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> so <math id="S2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{\infty}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{\infty}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> contains the entire <math id="S2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2g-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2g-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-skeleton of <math id="S2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The name “arc complex at infinity” calls for some explanation. A very nice discussion is given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha88</a>, §2]</cite>. The idea is this. Using a fundamental construction of Jenkins and Strebel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sr84</a>]</cite> it is possible to associate to each point in Teichmüller space <math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> (the space of finite area marked complete hyperbolic metrics on the surface <math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}\smallsetminus\{\ast\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}\smallsetminus\{\ast\}</annotation></semantics></math>) an embedded metric graph in <math id="S2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}\smallsetminus\{\ast\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}\smallsetminus\{\ast\}</annotation></semantics></math>. One can then homeomorphically identify <math id="S2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\smallsetminus\mathcal{A}_{\infty}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\smallsetminus\mathcal{A}_{\infty}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> using the arc system in <math id="S2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> dual to this graph (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha88</a>, §2]</cite> for details). Thus one may think of <math id="S2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{\infty}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{\infty}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> as extra “points at infinity” attached to Teichmüller space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Harer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha86</a>, Theorem 3.4]</cite> defined a continuous map <math id="S2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi:\mathcal{A}_{\infty}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\to\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi:\mathcal{A}_{\infty}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\to\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and showed that it is a homotopy equivalence (see §<a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> below for more on this map). Hence, from Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 2.2. ‣ 2 The Steinberg module, the curve complex and the arc complex ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> we get another characterization of the Steinberg module</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\cong\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{2g-2}(\mathcal{A}_{\infty}(\Sigma_{g}^{1});\mathbf{Z})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\cong\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{2g-2}(\mathcal{A}_{\infty}(\Sigma_{g}^{1});\mathbf{Z}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>A resolution of the Steinberg module</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we will give a resolution of the Steinberg module <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> as a <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-module.
The approach here is analogous to Ash’s simplification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">As94</a>, §1]</cite> of Lee and Szczarba’s resolution of the Steinberg module for <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">LS76</a>, §3]</cite> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu00a</a>]</cite> for a nice summary).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We will need the following two results of Harer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Harer)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The arc complex <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is contractible.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Harer)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The arc complex at infinity <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{\infty}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{\infty}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is homotopy equivalent to a wedge of spheres of dimension <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Harer). ‣ 3 A resolution of the Steinberg module ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> was established in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha85</a>, Theorem 1.5]</cite> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ht91</a>]</cite> for a concise proof) and
Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2 (Harer). ‣ 3 A resolution of the Steinberg module ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> was shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha86</a>, Theorem 3.3]</cite>. An alternate proof is provided in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Iv87</a>, Theorem 6.6]</cite></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Consider this portion of the long exact sequence of reduced homology groups for the pair of spaces <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{A},\mathcal{A}_{\infty})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{A},\mathcal{A}_{\infty})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{H}_{k+1}(\mathcal{A};\mathbf{Z})\to\mathrm{H}_{k+1}(\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})\to\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{k}(\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})\to\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{k}(\mathcal{A};\mathbf{Z})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{H}_{k+1}(\mathcal{A};\mathbf{Z})\to\mathrm{H}_{k+1}(\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})\to\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{k}(\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})\to\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{k}(\mathcal{A};\mathbf{Z}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">By Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Harer). ‣ 3 A resolution of the Steinberg module ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> the first and last groups in this sequence are trivial for <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>; consequently,</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{H}_{k+1}(\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})\cong\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{k}(\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})\ \ \ \ \mbox{for $k\geq 0$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{H}_{k+1}(\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})\cong\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{k}(\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})\ \ \ \ \mbox{for $k\geq 0$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Now combining equations (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2 The Steinberg module, the curve complex and the arc complex ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3 A resolution of the Steinberg module ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) we arrive at a very useful description of the Steinberg module,</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\cong\mathrm{H}_{2g-1}(\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\cong\mathrm{H}_{2g-1}(\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The chain complex <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\mathrm{C}_{\ast}(\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z}),\partial)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathrm{C}_{\ast}(\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z}),\partial)</annotation></semantics></math> for cellular homology of the space <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> will provide us with a resolution for <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> as a <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-module. We define the chain complex <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{\ast},\partial)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{\ast},\partial)</annotation></semantics></math> to be the shifted complex with</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{k}:=\mathrm{C}_{2g-1+k}(\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{k}:=\mathrm{C}_{2g-1+k}(\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">and the same boundary maps as <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\mathrm{C}_{\ast}(\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z}),\partial)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathrm{C}_{\ast}(\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z}),\partial)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{\ast},\partial)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{\ast},\partial)</annotation></semantics></math> be the chain complex defined in Equation (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3 A resolution of the Steinberg module ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). The exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\mathcal{F}_{4g-3}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\partial}}{{\to}}\cdots\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\partial}}{{\to}}\mathcal{F}_{2}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\partial}}{{\to}}\mathcal{F}_{1}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\partial}}{{\to}}\mathcal{F}_{0}\to\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\to 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mover><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo>∂</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mover><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo>∂</mo></mover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mo>∂</mo></mover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mo>∂</mo></mover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mathcal{F}_{4g-3}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\partial}}{{\to}}\cdots\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\partial}}{{\to}}\mathcal{F}_{2}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\partial}}{{\to}}\mathcal{F}_{1}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\partial}}{{\to}}\mathcal{F}_{0}\to\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\to 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is a finite <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-module resolution<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">1</span></span>
                
                
                
              <span id="footnote1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">Compare with the resolution of the Steinberg module for </span><math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> given in </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="footnote1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">As94</a><span id="footnote1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">, §1]</span></cite><span id="footnote1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">.</span></span></span></span> for the Steinberg module <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2 (Harer). ‣ 3 A resolution of the Steinberg module ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> the arc complex at infinity <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> has the homotopy type of a wedge of <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2g-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2g-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional spheres. Therefore</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathrm{H}_{k}(\mathcal{F}_{\ast})&amp;=\mathrm{H}_{2g-1+k}(\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})\\
&amp;\cong\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{2g-2+k}(\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})\\
&amp;=\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}0,&amp;k&gt;0\\
\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1}),&amp;k=0.\end{array}\right.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathrm{H}_{k}(\mathcal{F}_{\ast})&amp;=\mathrm{H}_{2g-1+k}(\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})\\
&amp;\cong\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{2g-2+k}(\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})\\
&amp;=\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}0,&amp;k&gt;0\\
\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1}),&amp;k=0.\end{array}\right.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">In other words, the chain complex <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{*},\partial)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{*},\partial)</annotation></semantics></math> gives a resolution of <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">The maximum number of arcs in an arc system occurs when the arcs comprise the one-skeleton of a one-vertex triangulation of <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using euler characteristic one may then conclude that an arc system has at most <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6g-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6g-3</annotation></semantics></math> arcs so <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and hence <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6g-4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6g-4</annotation></semantics></math> dimensional. It follows that <math id="S3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{k}=\mathrm{C}_{2g-1+k}(\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{k}=\mathrm{C}_{2g-1+k}(\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})=0</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;6g-4-2g+1=4g-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;6g-4-2g+1=4g-3</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">A <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system corresponds to a unique <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2g+k-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2g+k-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-cell in <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> with the cell decomposition inherited from <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. When the orientation of this <math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2g+k-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2g+k-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-cell is important we will specify it via an orientation on the unique <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2g+k-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2g+k-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-simplex in <math id="S3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> mapping to it. An <em id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">oriented</em> <math id="S3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system will be a <math id="S3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system together with an order on the set of arcs in the system up to alternating permutations. By definition <math id="S3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{k}=\mathrm{C}_{2g-1+k}(\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{k}=\mathrm{C}_{2g-1+k}(\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of finite <math id="S3.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear combinations of oriented <math id="S3.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          Translational Note: For the mapping class group, <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems play a similar role to that of <em id="footnote2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">modular symbols</em> for <math id="footnote2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">There are a finite number of topologically distinct ways to glue the sides of polygons to get a one-vertex cell decomposition of a surface of genus <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, and by the usual change of coordinates principle <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is transitive on the set of cell decompositions of the same topological type. Consequently, as a <math id="S3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-module, <math id="S3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by a finite number of oriented <math id="S3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Note that in general <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is not quite a free <math id="S3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-module since some filling arc systems have nontrivial (but always finite cyclic) stabilizers in <math id="S3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">There are two modifications either of which makes the resolution in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3.3. ‣ 3 A resolution of the Steinberg module ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> <span id="S3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">projective</span>. Firstly, if coefficients are taken in <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> then Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3.3. ‣ 3 A resolution of the Steinberg module ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> does give a projective resolution of the rational homology of the curve complex (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Br82</a>, pg. 30 Exercise 4]</cite>). Alternatively, if one restricts to some torsion-free subgroup <math id="S3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma&lt;\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma&lt;\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> then Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3.3. ‣ 3 A resolution of the Steinberg module ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> gives a free <math id="S3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-module resolution of <math id="S3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">It is worth pointing out that in the cases where the <math id="S3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-module resolution of <math id="S3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3.3. ‣ 3 A resolution of the Steinberg module ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> is projective, we get the following formula for computing group cohomology of <math id="S3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma&lt;\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma&lt;\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is torsion free and finite-index subgroup then for any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{H}^{n}(\Gamma;A)\cong\mathrm{H}_{4g-3-n}(\mathcal{F}_{\ast}\otimes_{\Gamma}A)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{H}^{n}(\Gamma;A)\cong\mathrm{H}_{4g-3-n}(\mathcal{F}_{\ast}\otimes_{\Gamma}A)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof is immediate from duality and properties of <math id="S3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Tor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Tor</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Tor}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By duality</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{H}^{n}(\Gamma;A)\cong\mathrm{H}_{4g-3-n}(\Gamma;\mathrm{St}\otimes_{\mathbf{Z}}A)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>St</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></msub><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{H}^{n}(\Gamma;A)\cong\mathrm{H}_{4g-3-n}(\Gamma;\mathrm{St}\otimes_{\mathbf{Z}}A)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}\otimes_{\mathbf{Z}}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></msub><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}\otimes_{\mathbf{Z}}A</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-module structure given by the diagonal action: <math id="S3.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(d\otimes a)=\gamma d\otimes\gamma a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(d\otimes a)=\gamma d\otimes\gamma a</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{H}_{4g-3-n}(\Gamma;\mathrm{St}\otimes_{\mathbf{Z}}A)\cong\mathrm{Tor}_{4g-3-n}^{\Gamma}(\mathrm{St},A)\cong\mathrm{H}_{4g-3-n}(\mathcal{F}_{\ast}\otimes_{\Gamma}A)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>St</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></msub><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Tor</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>St</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{H}_{4g-3-n}(\Gamma;\mathrm{St}\otimes_{\mathbf{Z}}A)\cong\mathrm{Tor}_{4g-3-n}^{\Gamma}(\mathrm{St},A)\cong\mathrm{H}_{4g-3-n}(\mathcal{F}_{\ast}\otimes_{\Gamma}A).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p13.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p13.3.1" class="ltx_text">See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Br82</a>, III.2]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma&lt;\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma&lt;\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is a torsion free and finite-index subgroup then the resolution in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3.3. ‣ 3 A resolution of the Steinberg module ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> is the shortest possible projective <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-module resolution of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p14.1" class="ltx_p">For the purposes of this paper we will make do with the resolution as is. Note that the tail of this resolution almost provides us with a <math id="S3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-module presentation for <math id="S3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. It is not exactly a presentation because <math id="S3.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a free <math id="S3.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-module. However, it can easily be augmented to give a <math id="S3.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-module presentation by artificially adding stabilizer relations which account for the accidental symmetries of certain <math id="S3.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Choose a set of oriented representatives <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=\{\phi_{0},\cdots,\phi_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\{\phi_{0},\cdots,\phi_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> of each <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit of the set of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems. For each <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}\in G</annotation></semantics></math> let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}\in\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}\in\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> be a generator of the stabilizer of the arc system for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}=\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}=\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math> be the sign of the permutation that the mapping class <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> induces on this set of arcs.
Also choose a set of oriented representatives <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\rho_{0},\cdots,\rho_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\rho_{0},\cdots,\rho_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> of each <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit of the set of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems. <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> has a presentation</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\cong\big\langle\phi_{0},\cdots,\phi_{n}\ \big|\ \partial\rho_{0},\cdots,\partial\rho_{m},(1-e_{0}h_{0})\phi_{0},\cdots,(1-e_{n}h_{n})\phi_{n}\big\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.500em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\cong\big\langle\phi_{0},\cdots,\phi_{n}\ \big|\ \partial\rho_{0},\cdots,\partial\rho_{m},(1-e_{0}h_{0})\phi_{0},\cdots,(1-e_{n}h_{n})\phi_{n}\big\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Forgetting the marked point gives a surjective ring homomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\twoheadrightarrow\mathbf{Z}\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↠</mo><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\twoheadrightarrow\mathbf{Z}\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If coefficients in the above presentation are sent to their images under this ring homomorphism then we get a <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>-module presentation</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})\cong\big\langle\phi_{0},\cdots,\phi_{n}\ \big|\ \partial\rho_{0},\cdots,\partial\rho_{m},(1-e_{0}h_{0})\phi_{0},\cdots,(1-e_{n}h_{n})\phi_{n}\big\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.500em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})\cong\big\langle\phi_{0},\cdots,\phi_{n}\ \big|\ \partial\rho_{0},\cdots,\partial\rho_{m},(1-e_{0}h_{0})\phi_{0},\cdots,(1-e_{n}h_{n})\phi_{n}\big\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3.3. ‣ 3 A resolution of the Steinberg module ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> we have the <math id="S3.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-module isomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\cong\frac{\mathcal{F}_{0}}{\partial\mathcal{F}_{1}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mfrac><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\cong\frac{\mathcal{F}_{0}}{\partial\mathcal{F}_{1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p15.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p15.2.1" class="ltx_text">By definition <math id="S3.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is spanned by oriented <math id="S3.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems as a <math id="S3.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-module. Every oriented <math id="S3.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system is of the form <math id="S3.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm h\phi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm h\phi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S3.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}\in G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S3.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> spans <math id="S3.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> as a <math id="S3.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-module. The only <math id="S3.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-linear dependencies in the set <math id="S3.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> arise from stabilizers of <math id="S3.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems. The arc system for a <math id="S3.p15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system cuts the surface into a single <math id="S3.p15.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4g</annotation></semantics></math>-gon, so its stabilizer must be a subgroup of the rotational symmetries of the regular <math id="S3.p15.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4g</annotation></semantics></math>-gon. Hence we have the presentation</span></p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{0}\cong\big\langle\phi_{0},\cdots,\phi_{n}\ \big|\ (1-e_{0}h_{0})\phi_{0},\cdots,(1-e_{n}h_{n})\phi_{n}\big\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{0}\cong\big\langle\phi_{0},\cdots,\phi_{n}\ \big|\ (1-e_{0}h_{0})\phi_{0},\cdots,(1-e_{n}h_{n})\phi_{n}\big\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p15.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p15.3.1" class="ltx_text">and the presentation for <math id="S3.p15.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> then follows.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the closed surface <math id="S3.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> we do not have a resolution for <math id="S3.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> as a <math id="S3.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>-module; however, we do get a <math id="S3.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>-module presentation for <math id="S3.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> by taking co-invariants (defined below) in the presentation for <math id="S3.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P&lt;\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P&lt;\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> be the <em id="S3.p16.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">point pushing subgroup</em> of <math id="S3.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>; that is, the kernel of <math id="S3.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> in the Birman Exact Sequence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bi69</a>]</cite></span></p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\to\pi_{1}(\Sigma_{g})\to\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\mu}}{{\to}}\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})\to 1\ \ \ \ \ \mbox{(for $g\geq 2$)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>μ</mi></mover><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:2.5em;" width="2.5em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>(for </mtext><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\to\pi_{1}(\Sigma_{g})\to\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\mu}}{{\to}}\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})\to 1\ \ \ \ \ \mbox{(for $g\geq 2$)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p16.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p16.2.1" class="ltx_text">or for <math id="S3.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> the trivial kernel of <math id="S3.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> in the exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\to\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{1}^{1})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\mu}}{{\cong}}\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{1})\to 1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo>≅</mo><mi>μ</mi></mover><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\to\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{1}^{1})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\mu}}{{\cong}}\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{1})\to 1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p16.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p16.3.1" class="ltx_text">The <math id="S3.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>-co-invariants <math id="S3.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> of a <math id="S3.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S3.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> are the quotient of <math id="S3.p16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> by the <math id="S3.p16.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>-submodule generated by the set <math id="S3.p16.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{pm-m|p\in P,m\in M\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{pm-m|p\in P,m\in M\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="S3.p16.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>-co-invariants <math id="S3.p16.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> have a <math id="S3.p16.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>-module structure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Br82</a>, §II.2 Problem 3]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">The exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{1}\to\mathcal{F}_{0}\to\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\to 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{1}\to\mathcal{F}_{0}\to\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\to 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p17.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p17.2.1" class="ltx_text">remains exact <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Br82</a>, §II.2]</cite> after taking <math id="S3.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>-co-invariants to get</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{1})_{P}\to(\mathcal{F}_{0})_{P}\to\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})_{P}\to 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{1})_{P}\to(\mathcal{F}_{0})_{P}\to\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})_{P}\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p17.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p17.3.1" class="ltx_text">By Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary 2.4. ‣ 2 The Steinberg module, the curve complex and the arc complex ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> and Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 2.3 (Harer). ‣ 2 The Steinberg module, the curve complex and the arc complex ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> we have <math id="S3.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>-module isomorphisms</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})_{P}\cong\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\cong\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})_{P}\cong\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\cong\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p17.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p17.4.1" class="ltx_text">Hence, as a <math id="S3.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S3.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})\cong\frac{(\mathcal{F}_{0})_{P}}{\partial(\mathcal{F}_{1})_{P}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})\cong\frac{(\mathcal{F}_{0})_{P}}{\partial(\mathcal{F}_{1})_{P}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p17.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p17.5.1" class="ltx_text">All that is left is to observe that taking the <math id="S3.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>-co-invariants of <math id="S3.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves the same effect as sending the coefficients in the presentation in (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 A resolution of the Steinberg module ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) to their images in <math id="S3.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>The Steinberg module is cyclic</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Part one of the Solomon-Tits Theorem states that the Tits building <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> has the homotopy type of a wedge of spheres of dimension <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-2</annotation></semantics></math>. The second part says that the <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(n)=\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{n-2}(\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q});\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(n)=\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{n-2}(\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q});\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is a cyclic <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>-module generated by the <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-sphere coming from a single apartment. In analogy with the first part of the Solomon-Tits Theorem, Harer has shown that the curve complex has the homotopy type of a wedge of <math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2g-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2g-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-spheres.
In this section we will prove the analog of the second part of the Solomon-Tits Theorem in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2 (The Steinberg module is cyclic). ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> below which states that as a <math id="S4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>-module the Steinberg module <math id="S4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by a single element.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, our Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2 (The Steinberg module is cyclic). ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> more closely resembles a result of Ash-Rudolph <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">AR79</a>, Theorem 4.1]</cite> which implies that the reduced homology of the Tits building <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is a cyclic <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>-module. The action of <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is no longer transitive on the set of apartments of <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>, so one must rely on a reduction process to rewrite the homology class of the sphere for an arbitrary apartment as a sum of homology classes of spheres of “integral unimodular” apartments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The arc complex at infinity does not come with any apartment structure, but <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems come in a finite number of types based on their <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-orbits. Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 4.6. ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a> below gives a reduction algorithm to write the class of any oriented <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system in the Steinberg module for the mapping class group as a linear combination of classes of oriented <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems of a single type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In order to state and prove Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2 (The Steinberg module is cyclic). ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> we will first discuss a notational convenience which will allow us to keep track of complicated filling arc systems.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Chord diagrams</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Already in genus two it is difficult to keep track of filling arc systems and their symmetries when drawn on surfaces. Chord diagrams provide a convenient notation for this. (See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mo94</a>, Figure 9]</cite> and the related discussion for an introduction.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">An (unlabelled) <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">chord diagram</em> (see Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1 Chord diagrams ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, left) will be a regular <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>-gon (which will always be depicted as a circle) with vertices paired off so that no two adjacent vertices are paired. The pairing will be indicated by <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">chords</em> in the chord diagram. We will call an edge of the <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>-gon an <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">outer edge</em> of the chord diagram. A <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">labelled chord diagram</em> (see Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4.1 Chord diagrams ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, right) will be a chord diagram in which each chord is labelled on one side by an element of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}(\Sigma_{g},\ast)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}(\Sigma_{g},\ast)</annotation></semantics></math> and on the other side by its inverse. In practice we will only label one side of each chord with the assumption that the other side is labelled with the inverse element in <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}(\Sigma_{g},\ast)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}(\Sigma_{g},\ast)</annotation></semantics></math>. Two labelled or unlabelled chord diagrams are the same if they can be made to agree after rotation of the <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>-gon. A labelling of a chord diagram will be called <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">proper</em> if it comes from a filling arc system in the surface <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We implicitly require all labellings of chord diagrams to be proper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In an arc system on the surface <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, arcs leave and return to a neighborhood of the marked point <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ast" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ast</annotation></semantics></math> in a certain cyclic order. To each filling arc system in <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> arcs, associate the labelled chord diagram obtained by placing a <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>-gon in a neighborhood of the marked point so that each arc enters and leaves the <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>-gon at a vertex. For each arc in the arc system connect the two vertices of the <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>-gon on that arc with a chord. The two sides of the chord correspond to the two sides of the arc. Label each side of the chord by the element of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}(\Sigma_{g},\ast)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}(\Sigma_{g},\ast)</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by following the arc on the corresponding side in a counter-clockwise direction (see Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4.1 Chord diagrams ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S4.F1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:319.8pt;height:118.1pt;vertical-align:-43.5pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(6.5pt,-2.4pt) scale(1.0421267294527,1.0421267294527) ;">
<div id="S4.F1.2.1" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:202.4pt;"><img src="/html/0711.0011/assets/filling-arcs-example.png" id="S4.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" width="336" height="171" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="S4.F1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="100.00" height="100.00" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,100.00) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(-149.44,73.34)"></g><g transform="translate(-269.82,76.1)"></g><g transform="translate(-175.73,51.2)"></g><g transform="translate(-96.86,123.15)"></g><g transform="translate(-153.59,20.76)"></g></g></svg></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.F1.2.2" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:101.2pt;"><img src="/html/0711.0011/assets/chords-example.png" id="S4.F1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" width="165" height="165" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="S4.F1.2.2.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F1.pic2" class="ltx_picture"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="100.00" height="100.00" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,100.00) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(-124.53,40.13)"></g><g transform="translate(-96.86,34.59)"></g><g transform="translate(-89.94,83.02)"></g><g transform="translate(-80.25,66.42)"></g><g transform="translate(-62.27,107.93)"></g><g transform="translate(-77.49,107.93)"></g><g transform="translate(-74.72,27.67)"></g><g transform="translate(-62.27,16.6)"></g></g></svg></span></p>
</div>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>A filling arc system (left) and the corresponding labelled chord diagram (right).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We will do most of our calculations using chord diagrams, so it will be convenient to be able to recover certain characteristics of the surface and embedded filling arc system corresponding to a given chord diagram. Firstly we briefly explain how to reconstruct the surface. From an <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-chord diagram we may construct a surface with a certain embedded, one-vertex graph as follows. A <em id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fat graph</em> is a regular neighborhood of a graph embedded in a surface together with the embedding of the graph, or equivalently, a graph together with, for each vertex of the graph, a cyclic order on the termini of the edges incident with that vertex. A chord diagram specifies a one-vertex fat graph with one edge for each chord, where the cyclic order on the edge termini corresponds to the cyclic order on endpoints of the chords in the diagram (see Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1 Chord diagrams ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S4.F2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:213.2pt;height:82.9pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(14.6pt,-5.7pt) scale(1.15862303613126,1.15862303613126) ;">
<div id="S4.F2.2.1" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:101.2pt;"><img src="/html/0711.0011/assets/fat-graph-1.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" width="165" height="165" alt="Refer to caption">
</div>
<div id="S4.F2.2.2" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:79.5pt;"><img src="/html/0711.0011/assets/fat-graph-2.png" id="S4.F2.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" width="126" height="153" alt="Refer to caption">
</div>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>A chord diagram (left) and the corresponding fat graph (right).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Now we may glue disks to each of the <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> boundary components of the fat graph to get a closed surface in which the edges of the fat graph form a filling arc system. Note that one generally considers the fat graph dual to an arc system <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">PM07</a>]</cite> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">ABP09</a>]</cite>, but here our arc system and fat graph agree. It will be useful to know the genus <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> of this closed surface, which is easily calculated using the number <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> of edges in the fat graph and the number <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> of boundary components of the fat graph, to be</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=\frac{n+1-b}{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=\frac{n+1-b}{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">In particular, this shows that a chord diagram with <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g</annotation></semantics></math> chords corresponds to a <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system in a surface of genus <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> precisely when the corresponding fat graph has one boundary component.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In light of Equation (<a href="#S4.E11" title="In 4.1 Chord diagrams ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) above we would like to be able to quickly read off the number <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> of boundary components in the fat graph corresponding to a given chord diagram. In fact it is easy to see that <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of <em id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cycles</em> in the chord diagram. A <span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cycle</span> in a chord diagram (see Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.1 Chord diagrams ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) is an alternating sequence of chords and outer edges of the chord diagram obtained by starting at a point just inside an outer edge of the diagram and walking along in a clockwise direction keeping the outer edge on one’s left until a chord is encountered, turning right and then following the chord keeping it on the left until an outer edge is encountered, turning right and repeating until one returns to the starting point.
Notice that for each cycle in a labelled chord diagram the products of the labels in the cycle must be <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\in\pi_{1}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\in\pi_{1}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>, since the corresponding loop in the surface bounds a disk.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S4.F3.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:319.8pt;height:70.5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(17.3pt,-3.8pt) scale(1.12134993585345,1.12134993585345) ;">
<div id="S4.F3.2.1" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:94.0pt;"><img src="/html/0711.0011/assets/cycle-1.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" width="147" height="145" alt="Refer to caption">
</div>
<div id="S4.F3.2.2" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:94.0pt;"><img src="/html/0711.0011/assets/cycle-2.png" id="S4.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" width="147" height="145" alt="Refer to caption">
</div>
<div id="S4.F3.2.3" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:94.0pt;"><img src="/html/0711.0011/assets/cycle-3.png" id="S4.F3.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" width="147" height="145" alt="Refer to caption">
</div>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Cycles in chord diagrams.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Two chords are <em id="S4.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">parallel</em> if along with two outer edges they bound a rectangular cycle. For example, the left chord diagram in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.1 Chord diagrams ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> has two parallel chords. Since we will only be concerned with arc systems with no parallel arcs, we will not consider chord diagrams with parallel chords.
We may identify <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems for the surface <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with (properly) labelled chord diagrams with <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+k</annotation></semantics></math> chords, <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> cycles and no parallel chords.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">As an oriented <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system gives an oriented cell in <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>, we may take its boundary in the chain complex <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> by taking the boundary of the corresponding oriented simplex in <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and projecting that boundary back to <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>. The boundary of a simplex is an alternating sum of the codimension-1 faces. Thus the boundary of a <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> will be a linear combination of all the <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems obtained from <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> by removing one arc. Removing certain arcs from <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> may leave an arc system which no longer fills <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The simplex of <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> for such an arc system is contained in <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> and hence is trivial in <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the language of chord diagrams we will be able to tell when this has happened by counting cycles and applying Equation (<a href="#S4.E11" title="In 4.1 Chord diagrams ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The mapping class group <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> acts on the fundamental group of the closed surface of genus <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. This gives a left action of <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> on labelled chord diagrams by modifying the labels. This action of <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> preserves the underlying unlabelled chord diagram for a <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system. Conversely, by the usual change of coordinates principle, any homeomorphism of fat graphs extends to a homeomorphism of the surface so the mapping class group is transitive on the set of proper labellings for a fixed unlabelled chord diagram. Thus the <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-orbits of <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems correspond precisely to unlabelled chord diagrams with <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+k</annotation></semantics></math> chords and <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> cycles.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 Chord diagrams ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> depicts the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> orbits of the action of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> on the set of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems for the surface, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{2}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{2}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. These are all <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-chord diagrams with one cycle and (redundantly) no parallel chords.</p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S4.F4.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:319.8pt;height:51.7pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(28.1pt,-4.6pt) scale(1.21362462345031,1.21362462345031) ;">
<div id="S4.F4.2.1" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:65.0pt;"><img src="/html/0711.0011/assets/g2-1.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" width="98" height="98" alt="Refer to caption">
</div>
<div id="S4.F4.2.2" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:65.0pt;"><img src="/html/0711.0011/assets/g2-2.png" id="S4.F4.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" width="98" height="98" alt="Refer to caption">
</div>
<div id="S4.F4.2.3" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:65.0pt;"><img src="/html/0711.0011/assets/g2-3.png" id="S4.F4.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" width="98" height="98" alt="Refer to caption">
</div>
<div id="S4.F4.2.4" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:65.0pt;"><img src="/html/0711.0011/assets/g2-4.png" id="S4.F4.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" width="98" height="98" alt="Refer to caption">
</div>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>The unlabelled <math id="S4.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-chord diagrams corresponding to the four <math id="S4.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-orbits of <math id="S4.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems in the genus <math id="S4.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> surface with one marked point.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in §<a href="#S3" title="3 A resolution of the Steinberg module ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> we have the <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-module isomorphism from Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 3.6. ‣ 3 A resolution of the Steinberg module ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a></p>
<table id="S4.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\cong\frac{\mathcal{F}_{0}}{\partial\mathcal{F}_{1}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mfrac><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\cong\frac{\mathcal{F}_{0}}{\partial\mathcal{F}_{1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Although <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are finitely generated <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-modules, the number of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-orbits of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems grows very quickly as a function of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">HZ86</a>]</cite>). It is therefore somewhat surprising that the Steinberg module is generated by the class of a single <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(The Steinberg module is cyclic)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}\in\mathcal{F}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}\in\mathcal{F}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system given in Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\phi_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\phi_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math> be the class of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})=\mathcal{F}_{0}/\partial\mathcal{F}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})=\mathcal{F}_{0}/\partial\mathcal{F}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated as a <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-module by <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\phi_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\phi_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math>. For the closed surface let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\phi_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\phi_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math> be the class of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})=(\mathcal{F}_{0})_{P}/\partial(\mathcal{F}_{1})_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})=(\mathcal{F}_{0})_{P}/\partial(\mathcal{F}_{1})_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. proof of Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 3.6. ‣ 3 A resolution of the Steinberg module ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>). Similarly, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated as a <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>-module by <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\phi_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\phi_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2 (The Steinberg module is cyclic). ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> will be proved using Propositions <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 4.6. ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a> and <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Proposition 4.5. ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\phi_{0}]\in\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\phi_{0}]\in\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (resp. <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\phi_{0}]\in\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\phi_{0}]\in\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>) is nontrivial for <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Corollary 4.3. ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\phi_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\phi_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math> were trivial then by Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2 (The Steinberg module is cyclic). ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> the Steinberg module <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> would be trivial. But then <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> would have a finite index subgroup <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> whose dualizing module is trivial. This would imply that <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> must be the trivial group and hence that <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is finite (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">IJ08</a>]</cite> for more).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="/html/0711.0011/assets/generator.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" width="223" height="223" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S4.F5.2" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F5.pic1" class="ltx_picture"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="100.00" height="100.00" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,100.00) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(-85.79,113.46)"></g><g transform="translate(-71.95,91.32)"></g><g transform="translate(-60.88,63.65)"></g><g transform="translate(-69.19,38.74)"></g><g transform="translate(-110.7,113.46)"></g><g transform="translate(-127.3,91.32)"></g><g transform="translate(-136.99,63.65)"></g><g transform="translate(-127.3,38.74)"></g><g transform="translate(-109.31,0)"></g><g transform="translate(-98.24,-33.21)"></g></g></svg></span></p></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>The generator for <math id="S4.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A natural first guess at a single generator for <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is the arc system coming from the standard identification of the the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4g</annotation></semantics></math>-gon which corresponds to the labelled chord diagram in Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, but in fact by Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Proposition 4.5. ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> below, the class of this <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system is trivial in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="/html/0711.0011/assets/guess.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" width="223" height="223" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S4.F6.2" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F6.pic1" class="ltx_picture"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="100.00" height="100.00" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,100.00) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(-85.79,113.46)"></g><g transform="translate(-60.88,91.32)"></g><g transform="translate(-49.81,60.88)"></g><g transform="translate(-66.42,31.83)"></g><g transform="translate(-110.7,113.46)"></g><g transform="translate(-136.99,88.56)"></g><g transform="translate(-146.67,58.12)"></g><g transform="translate(-127.3,30.44)"></g><g transform="translate(-109.31,0)"></g></g></svg></span></p></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>A trivial class in <math id="S4.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A filling arc system describes a decomposition of the surface <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> into polygons. If any of the polygons has more than <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> vertices, one may add an arc connecting two non-adjacent vertices of that polygon to get a filling arc system with one more arc. This same process can be done from the point of view of chord diagrams. Given a <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> represented as a labelled chord diagram, the outer edges of <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> are partitioned into <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint sets (corresponding to the polygons mentioned above) according to which of the <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> cycles they belong to. A <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system can be created by adding a new chord (not parallel to any of the chords of <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>) connecting any two outer edges of <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> in the same cycle. Note that since a <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system has a single cycle, <em id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">any</em> new (non-parallel) chord gives a <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Two chords in a chord diagram <em id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cross</em> if the cyclic order on their endpoints forces them to. The finest partition of the set of chords in which each pair of crossing chords is in the same set will give the <em id="S4.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">connected components</em> of the chord diagram. For example the chord diagram in Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> is connected, while the chord diagram in Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> has <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> connected components and so is disconnected for <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.
A cycle <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">traverses</span> a chord if it follows along the chord. Note that a cycle can traverse a chord 0, 1, or 2 times. Given a cycle <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and a chord <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> on a chord diagram <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> we will say that <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> remains on one side of <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math></span> if the cycle <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> never traverses any chords that cross <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. Each chord <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> in a chord diagram cuts the chord diagram into two sets whose closures are disks. Notice that the cycle <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> remains on one side of <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> precisely when <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in one of those disks. Finally note that each chord <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is either traversed twice by a single cycle which must also traverse a chord which crosses <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> or is traversed once by two different cycles.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is a disconnected <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\alpha]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha]</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})=\mathcal{F}_{0}/\partial\mathcal{F}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})=\mathcal{F}_{0}/\partial\mathcal{F}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Add a chord <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> which does not cross any of the chords of <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and such there are nonempty connected components of <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> on both sides of <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> be the resulting chord diagram. Firstly, we claim that <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system. This will be the case unless the chord <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is parallel to some chord <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that such a chord <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> exists. Then <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> crosses the same (empty) set of chords as <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> has a chord <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> which is traversed by two </span><span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">different</span><span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text"> cycles. Thus <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> has at least two cycles contradicting the assumption that <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now since <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system we may orient it and take its boundary. Next we claim</span></p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\alpha_{c}=\pm\alpha." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">±</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\alpha_{c}=\pm\alpha.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">By definition <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\alpha_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\alpha_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear combination of the <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems that one can obtain by removing a single chord from <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>. No chords of <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> cross the chord <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> so any chord diagram <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> by removing a chord other than <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> cannot have a single cycle which traverses <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> twice. Such a chord diagram <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> must have two different cycles which traverse <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be a <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system. Thus <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is the only <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system that one may get by removing a chord from <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>. Equation (<a href="#S4.E12" title="In Proof. ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) follows establishing the proposition.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Now we will introduce a subset of the <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems whose classes generate <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and for which, by Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Proposition 4.5. ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, the class of each element, save one, is trivial.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="/html/0711.0011/assets/salient.png" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" width="282" height="34" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S4.F7.2" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F7.pic1" class="ltx_picture"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="100.00" height="100.00" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,100.00) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(-127.3,5.53)"></g></g></svg></span></p></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>A connected component of a salient chord diagram</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">A chord diagram will be called <em id="S4.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">salient</em> if each of its connected components is of the form given in
Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.
Of course <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> is the unique connected, salient <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.6</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is generated by salient <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> a chord diagram will be said to have a <em id="S4.SS2.p10.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">salient tail of length <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math></em> if a neighborhood of some segment in the boundary of the diagram is homeomorphic to the neighborhood pictured in Figure <a href="#S4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Proof. ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="/html/0711.0011/assets/salient-tail.png" id="S4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" width="237" height="68" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S4.F8.2" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F8.pic1" class="ltx_picture"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="100.00" height="100.00" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,100.00) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(-166.04,30.44)"></g><g transform="translate(-98.24,5.53)"></g></g></svg></span></p></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>A salient tail of length <math id="S4.F8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> in a chord diagram</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given a <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> with a salient tail of length <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, we can add a chord <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> to the far right of the salient tail to get a <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> with a salient tail of length <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>. There is some <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq m\leq 2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq m\leq 2g</annotation></semantics></math> and there are <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq m</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial\alpha_{c}=\pm\alpha+\sum_{i=1}^{m}\pm\beta_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">±</mo><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em">±</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\alpha_{c}=\pm\alpha+\sum_{i=1}^{m}\pm\beta_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">Notice that if any single chord in the salient tail of <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> other than <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is removed from <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> then the resulting chord diagram is disconnected, and so by Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Proposition 4.5. ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> the class of that <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system is trivial in <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus for every <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq m</annotation></semantics></math> the class of the <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is either trivial in <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> or the chord diagram for <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has a salient tail of length <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Iterating this process recursively no more than <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g</annotation></semantics></math> times we may write the class of any <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system as a linear combination of classes of salient <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">With Propositions <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 4.6. ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a> and <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Proposition 4.5. ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> established, the proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2 (The Steinberg module is cyclic). ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> is quite short.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2 (The Steinberg module is cyclic). ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 4.6. ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>, <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by salient <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems. The element <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}\in\mathcal{F}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}\in\mathcal{F}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a representative of the unique <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit of connected, salient <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems. Thus by Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Proposition 4.5. ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\phi_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\phi_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math> generates <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the closed surface without a marked point <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})\cong\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})\cong\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-modules, so <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\phi_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\phi_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math> generates <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> as a <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-module. On the other hand, the <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-module structure of <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> factors through <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\phi_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\phi_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math> generates <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> as a <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>-module.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.7</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a consequence of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2 (The Steinberg module is cyclic). ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> there is a left ideal</p>
<table id="S4.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}\subset\mathbf{Z}\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}\subset\mathbf{Z}\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">such that the sequence of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>-modules</p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\mathcal{J}\to\mathbf{Z}\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})\to\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})\to 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mathcal{J}\to\mathbf{Z}\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})\to\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})\to 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.3" class="ltx_p">is exact. In theory, for a fixed genus <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> the presentation in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3.3. ‣ 3 A resolution of the Steinberg module ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> gives enough information to calculate a finite generating set for <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A more “geometric” understanding of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}</annotation></semantics></math> would certainly advance our understanding of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. At minimum one would like to know the stabilizer in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> of the class <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\phi_{0}]\in\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\phi_{0}]\in\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Spheres in the curve complex</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated above it is useful to view the Steinberg module as the reduced homology of the arc complex at infinity. On the other hand, the curve complexes <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> are more widely studied so we will now explain Harer’s homotopy equivalence <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> between the arc complex at infinity and the curve complex. We will use this map to get an explicit nontrivial <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-sphere in the curve complex of the surface of genus <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> (see Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 4.8. ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> below) which will motivate Conjecture <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem9" title="Conjecture 4.9. ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.9</span></a> below. We point out that results of Penner-McShane in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">PM07</a>]</cite> provide an alternative approach to converting a <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system into a sphere in the curve complex.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Harer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha86</a>, Theorem 3.4]</cite> gives a homotopy equivalence from the arc complex at infinity to the curve complex. Harer’s map</p>
<table id="S4.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi:\mathcal{A}_{\infty}^{\circ\circ}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\to\mathcal{C}^{\circ}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mo>∘</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∘</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi:\mathcal{A}_{\infty}^{\circ\circ}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})\to\mathcal{C}^{\circ}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">is simplicial, where <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{\infty}^{\circ\circ}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mo>∘</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∘</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{\infty}^{\circ\circ}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the second barycentric subdivision of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{\infty}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{\infty}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{\circ}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{\circ}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the first barycentric subdivision of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. The map <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> is defined on the vertices of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{\infty}^{\circ\circ}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mo>∘</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∘</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{\infty}^{\circ\circ}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, as follows. A vertex <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{\infty}^{\circ\circ}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mo>∘</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∘</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{\infty}^{\circ\circ}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is a nested sequence of non-filling arc systems <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}\subset\beta_{2}\subset\cdots\subset\beta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">⊂</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⊂</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}\subset\beta_{2}\subset\cdots\subset\beta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq k</annotation></semantics></math> if one removes a small open regular neighborhood of the union of the arcs in <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, one is left with a surface <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma(i)\subset\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma(i)\subset\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with nonempty boundary. After omitting redundancies and trivial curves, the boundary components of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma(i)</annotation></semantics></math> give a curve system <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(v):=\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(v):=\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> again omitting redundancies. In fact, <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(v)</annotation></semantics></math> is a curve system since if <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{i}\subset\beta_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{i}\subset\beta_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> then we can arrange that <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma(i)\supset\Sigma(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊃</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma(i)\supset\Sigma(j)</annotation></semantics></math> and hence the curves of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> can be taken to be disjoint from the curves of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Of course <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(v)</annotation></semantics></math> is a vertex of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{\circ}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{\circ}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> whose vertices are curve systems. We then extend <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> simplicially; that is, the simplex with vertices <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1},\cdots,v_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1},\cdots,v_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped linearly to the simplex with vertices <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(v_{1}),\cdots,\Psi(v_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(v_{1}),\cdots,\Psi(v_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> gives an arc system whose class in <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{H}_{2g-1}(\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{H}_{2g-1}(\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is nontrivial. From the long exact sequence for the homology of the pair of spaces <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{A},\mathcal{A}_{\infty})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{A},\mathcal{A}_{\infty})</annotation></semantics></math> we have the isomorphism</p>
<table id="S4.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{H}_{2g-1}(\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\partial}}{{\cong}}\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{2g-2}(\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo>≅</mo><mo>∂</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{H}_{2g-1}(\mathcal{A}/\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\partial}}{{\cong}}\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{2g-2}(\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">In <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> the arc system for <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> gives a <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2g-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2g-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-simplex all of whose proper subfaces are contained in <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{\infty}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{\infty}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. The class of <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial[\phi_{0}]\in\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{2g-2}(\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial[\phi_{0}]\in\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{2g-2}(\mathcal{A}_{\infty};\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by the boundary of this <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2g-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2g-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-simplex.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For the surface <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{2}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{2}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> we may compute the image of <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\phi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\phi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> directly. Figure <a href="#S4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> gives the image of the vertices of the first barycentric subdivision of <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\phi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\phi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F9" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S4.F9.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:319.8pt;height:290.5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(43.0pt,-39.0pt) scale(1.36737532367278,1.36737532367278) ;"><img src="/html/0711.0011/assets/psi-image.png" id="S4.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" width="531" height="490" alt="Refer to caption">
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>A nontrivial sphere in the barycentric subdivision <math id="S4.F9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{\circ}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{\circ}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> of the curve complex</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">One may easily fill in the images for vertices in the second barycentric subdivision by taking unions of the curve systems at the vertices of the first subdivision. The sphere in Figure <a href="#S4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> is nontrivial, but it is slightly unsatisfying in that it is specified in <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{\circ}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{\circ}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and not <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Figure <a href="#S4.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> and Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 4.8. ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> below provide a homotopic sphere with a nicer description. The reader may recognize the shape in Figure <a href="#S4.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> to be the boundary of the dual of the <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional associahedron (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Le89</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F10" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S4.F10.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:319.8pt;height:288.9pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(45.5pt,-41.1pt) scale(1.39760669548032,1.39760669548032) ;"><img src="/html/0711.0011/assets/sphere.png" id="S4.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" width="520" height="476" alt="Refer to caption">
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span>This is a homologically nontrivial sphere in <math id="S4.F10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Forgetting the marked point gives a homologically nontrivial sphere in <math id="S4.F10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.8</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit of the homology class of the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-sphere in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> pictured in Figure <a href="#S4.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> generates <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{H}_{2}(\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1});\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{H}_{2}(\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1});\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>. Forgetting the marked point gives a <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-sphere in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> whose <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit generates <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{H}_{2}(\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2});\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{H}_{2}(\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2});\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">The arc system for the generator <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\phi_{0}]\in\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\phi_{0}]\in\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> from Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2 (The Steinberg module is cyclic). ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> is pictured in Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4.1 Chord diagrams ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. One may use Harer’s map directly to show that the vertices in the first barycentric subdivision of <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\phi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\phi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> are mapped under <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> to the curve systems pictured in Figure <a href="#S4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. We will give a simplicial sphere in the unbarycentricly subdivided curve complex which is homotopic to the sphere pictured in Figure <a href="#S4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. One may construct a homotopy equivalence</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})\to\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})\to\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">as follows. Let <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of exactly those curves appearing in Figure <a href="#S4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. Fix some linear order on <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> so that each of the curves that appear in Figure <a href="#S4.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> is greater than all of those that do not. Let <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})\to\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})\to\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> be the
map satisfying the following three properties:</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The map <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> fixes the vertices of <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any simplex <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> whose vertices are disjoint from <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> the map <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f|_{\sigma}:\sigma\to\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f|_{\sigma}:\sigma\to\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the identity.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If at least one vertex of the simplex <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is in the set <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f|_{\sigma}:\sigma\to\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f|_{\sigma}:\sigma\to\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the map which is linear on the barycentric subdivision of <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, fixes the vertices of <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and sends the barycenter of <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> to the greatest vertex of <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> in the order on <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">The map <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})\to\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})\to\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is homotopic to the identity. In fact, sending each point of a simplex to weighted averages of itself and its image under <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> gives the homotopy between <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and the identity. It follows that the sphere <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\Psi(\partial\phi_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\Psi(\partial\phi_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is homotopic to <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(\partial\phi_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(\partial\phi_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. Some of the vertices in Figure <a href="#S4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> coalesce under <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and we get the simpler homotopic sphere pictured in Figure <a href="#S4.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>. We may then conclude that the <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{2}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-orbits of sphere in Figure <a href="#S4.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> generate <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{H}_{2}(\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1});\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{H}_{2}(\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2}^{1});\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>. Forgetting the marked point gives a <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-sphere in <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> whose <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit generates <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{H}_{2}(\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2});\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{H}_{2}(\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{2});\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha86</a>, Lemma 3.6]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The number of vertices in the boundary of the first barycentric subdivision of the <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2g-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2g-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-simplex is <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{2g}-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{2g}-2</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, for large <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> the direct approach to producing homologically nontrivial spheres in <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> given in the proof of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 4.8. ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> is impractical. Moreover, the asymmetry of the arc system for <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is likely to yield a sphere in <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> which is difficult to even describe in any concise form.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F11" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S4.F11.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:277.2pt;height:107.7pt;vertical-align:-4.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(39.1pt,-15.2pt) scale(1.3936763577731,1.3936763577731) ;"><img src="/html/0711.0011/assets/assoc.png" id="S4.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="419" height="171" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="S4.F11.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F11.pic1" class="ltx_picture"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="100.00" height="100.00" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,100.00) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(-149.44,65.03)"></g></g></svg></span></p>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 11: </span>Using these <math id="S4.F11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+2</annotation></semantics></math> curves one can construct a map from the boundary of the dual of the <math id="S4.F11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2g-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2g-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional associahedron into <math id="S4.F11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Is that map homologically nontrivial?</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Using the curves in Figure <a href="#S4.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> there is a simple construction of a map of a <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2g-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2g-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-sphere into <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math> be the boundary of the dual of the <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2g-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2g-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional associahedron which we now define. Fix a regular <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2g+2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2g+2)</annotation></semantics></math>-gon <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math> is the simplicial complex whose vertices are diagonals of <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> and whose simplices are given by sets of disjoint diagonals in <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. It is well-known that <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2g-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2g-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional sphere <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Le89</a>]</cite>. Associate the vertices of <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> with the <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+2</annotation></semantics></math> curves in Figure <a href="#S4.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> so that adjacent vertices of <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to intersecting curves. Each pair of nonintersecting curves in Figure <a href="#S4.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> corresponds to a diagonal of <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. Let</p>
<table id="S4.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}:\Theta\to\mathcal{C}^{\circ}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}:\Theta\to\mathcal{C}^{\circ}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p8.2" class="ltx_p">be the simplicial map sending each pair of nonintersecting curves in Figure <a href="#S4.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> to the curve system (with at most <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> nontrivial curves) consisting of the boundary curves of the surface got by removing an open regular neighborhood of the union of all the other curves. For each vertex <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math> choose a curve <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{v}\in q^{\prime}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{v}\in q^{\prime}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> and let</p>
<table id="S4.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:\Theta\to\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:\Theta\to\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p8.3" class="ltx_p">be the simplicial map sending <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. One may construct a homotopy like the one in the proof of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 4.8. ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> above to show that <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are homotopic and hence the homotopy class of <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of the choices of <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{v}\in q^{\prime}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{v}\in q^{\prime}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 4.9</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> the class <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[q]\in\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{2g-2}(\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1});\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[q]\in\widetilde{\mathrm{H}}_{2g-2}(\mathcal{C}(\Sigma_{g}^{1});\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is nontrivial. (When <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> the proper picture for Figure <a href="#S4.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> consists of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> pairs of parallel curves.)</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">By Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 4.8. ‣ 4.3 Spheres in the curve complex ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> above the conjecture holds for <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math>. It also holds<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            Of course the mapping class groups of the surfaces of genus <math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="footnote3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> are somewhat exceptional. (For instance they have nontrivial centers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Iv02</a>, Theorem 7.5.D]</cite>.)</span></span></span> for <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Questions for further study</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. The negative of the identity matrix <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-I\in\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-I\in\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> stabilizes every subspace of <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-I</annotation></semantics></math> is in the kernel of
the action of <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> on its Steinberg module <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently the action of <math id="S5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> factors though an action of the simple group <math id="S5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{PSL}(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PSL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{PSL}(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">La02</a>, Theorem 9.3]</cite>). Simple groups must either act trivially or faithfully. Since <math id="S5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> is a nontrivial <math id="S5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(n,\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>-module it must also be a nontrivial and hence faithful <math id="S5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{PSL}(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PSL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{PSL}(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>-module. It follows that <math id="S5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> is a faithful <math id="S5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{PSL}(n,\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PSL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{PSL}(n,\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>-module.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As we have seen in Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary 2.4. ‣ 2 The Steinberg module, the curve complex and the arc complex ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>, for <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> the kernel of the action of <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> on the Steinberg module contains the infinite point pushing subgroup <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P&lt;\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P&lt;\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. For the closed surface without a marked point there is some hope that the action of the mapping class group (modulo its center) on the Steinberg module is faithful.
When <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the hyperelliptic involution generates the center <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=Z(\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=Z(\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}))</annotation></semantics></math> of the mapping class group and acts trivially on the curve complex. For <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> the center <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=Z(\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=Z(\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}))</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Iv02</a>, Theorem 7.5.D]</cite>. Thus for <math id="S5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> the action of <math id="S5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> factors through <math id="S5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})/Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})/Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=Z(\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=Z(\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g}))</annotation></semantics></math> be the center of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. Is <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> a faithful <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})/Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})/Z</annotation></semantics></math>-module? If not what is the kernel of the action?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The Solomon-Tits Theorem (<span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cf.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">So69</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Br89</a>, §IV.5 Theorem 2]</cite>) shows that the Tits building <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(n,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> has the homotopy type of a wedge of spheres, that its reduced homology is a cyclic module (over the associated <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-group), and futher gives a <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-basis for the reduced homology. Harer has shown that the curve complex has the homotopy type of a wedge of spheres. Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2 (The Steinberg module is cyclic). ‣ 4.2 A singleton generating set for the Steinberg module ‣ 4 The Steinberg module is cyclic ‣ Homology of the curve complex and the Steinberg module of the mapping class group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> above shows that the reduced homology of the curve complex is a cyclic <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}(\Sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>-module. The full “Solomon-Tits Theorem” for the mapping class group should also provide the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Give a <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-basis for <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})\cong\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g})\cong\mathrm{St}(\Sigma_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Steinberg modules for automorphism groups of free groups.</span>
Let <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the free group on <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> generators, and let <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Aut}(F_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Aut}(F_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Out}(F_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Out</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Out}(F_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> denote its automorphism group and outer automorphism group respectively (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vo02</a>]</cite> for a survey of these groups). Many of the results on the homological structure of the mapping class group have analogs for <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Out}(F_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Out</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Out}(F_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Aut}(F_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Aut}(F_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, Bestvina and Feighn <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">BF00</a>]</cite> have shown that <math id="S5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Out}(F_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Out</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Out}(F_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Aut}(F_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Aut}(F_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> are virtual duality groups. To date, the module structure of the dualizing modules for (torsion-free, finite index subgroups of) these groups has not been studied in depth.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In the case of <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Aut}(F_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Aut}(F_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> Hatcher and Vogtmann <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">HV98</a>]</cite> have proposed a likely candidate for this dualizing module. They define a simplicial complex based on the poset of free factors of <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and show that it has the homotopy type of a wedge of spheres. They define its reduced homology group to be the Steinberg module of <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Aut}(F_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Aut}(F_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, and ask if this module provides the dualizing module for finite index, torsion-free subgroups of <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Aut}(F_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Aut}(F_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. It is possible that the approach of the current paper might be used to address this question.</p>
</div>
</section>
